<h2>Before filing: the preliminary checks</h2>

<p>Three screening questions decide whether a complaint is maintainable at all. First, is the complainant a <em>consumer</em> &mdash; did he buy goods or hire services for consideration otherwise than for a commercial purpose? Second, is the grievance one the Act recognises &mdash; a defect in goods, a deficiency in service, an unfair or restrictive trade practice, overcharging, or dangerous goods offered for sale? Third, is the complaint within limitation &mdash; two years from the cause of action under Section 69 of the Act?</p>

<p>A legal notice is not a statutory precondition, but a well-drafted notice frequently produces settlement, and it fixes the record on dates, defects and demands &mdash; useful later on limitation and interest.</p>

<h2>Choosing the forum</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Forum</th><th>Pecuniary jurisdiction</th><th>Where it sits</th></tr>
  <tr><td>District Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission</td><td>Consideration paid up to ₹50 lakh</td><td>Multiple District Commissions across Delhi</td></tr>
  <tr><td>State Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission</td><td>Above ₹50 lakh and up to ₹2 crore</td><td>Delhi State Commission, New Delhi</td></tr>
  <tr><td>National Consumer Disputes Redressal Commission (NCDRC)</td><td>Above ₹2 crore</td><td>New Delhi</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Two features of the 2019 Act deserve emphasis. Pecuniary jurisdiction turns on the <em>consideration paid</em> for the goods or services, not on the compensation claimed &mdash; a change from the 1986 regime that prevents inflation of claims to reach a higher forum. And territorial jurisdiction now includes the place where the <em>complainant</em> resides or personally works for gain, so a Delhi resident can ordinarily sue a distant builder or online seller from Delhi.</p>

<h2>Filing and the first hearing</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Draft the complaint.</strong> Parties, facts in chronological order, the defect or deficiency, the consideration paid (which fixes jurisdiction), limitation, and the reliefs — refund, replacement, compensation, discontinuance of the practice, costs. Supporting documents: invoice or agreement, payment proof, correspondence, notice and reply.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>File on e-Daakhil or physically.</strong> Consumer complaints in Delhi can be filed online through the e-Daakhil portal with scanned documents and online payment of the prescribed fee, or physically before the commission. Fees are modest and slab-based; for claims at the lowest slabs no fee is payable.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Scrutiny and admission.</strong> The commission examines maintainability — consumer status, jurisdiction, limitation. If admitted, notice issues to the opposite party to file its written version, ordinarily within 30 days of service, extendable by a further 15 days.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Evidence by affidavit.</strong> Both sides file evidence affidavits with documents; cross-examination is exceptional. Expert evidence — a laboratory analysis of goods, a medical board opinion — is directed where the dispute needs it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Arguments and order.</strong> After written and oral submissions the commission passes a reasoned order granting or refusing relief, with interest and litigation costs where warranted.</div>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p>The 2019 Act also builds in mediation. Where settlement appears possible and both parties consent, the commission may refer the dispute to its attached consumer mediation cell at the admission stage or later. A settlement recorded in mediation has the force of an order, and no appeal lies against it.</p>
</div>

<h2>Interim orders, appeals and execution</h2>

<p>Commissions can pass interim orders where goods are perishable or the opposite party is dissipating assets, though the jurisdiction is used sparingly. The appellate ladder is short and strictly timed: from the District Commission, an appeal lies to the State Commission within 45 days; from the State Commission&rsquo;s original orders, to the NCDRC within 30 days; and from the NCDRC&rsquo;s original orders, to the Supreme Court within 30 days. An appellant directed to pay under the order appealed from must ordinarily deposit half of that amount before the appeal is entertained &mdash; a provision that filters out purely dilatory appeals.</p>

<p>A final order that is not complied with is enforceable under Section 71 of the Act in the manner of a decree, and non-compliance is separately punishable under Section 72 with imprisonment and fine. In practice, execution applications before the Delhi commissions &mdash; particularly against builders and insurers &mdash; are a substantial docket of their own, and a decree-holder should move promptly once the compliance period in the order expires.</p>

<h2>Where complaints commonly fail</h2>

<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Commercial purchases dressed up as consumer transactions — a company buying machinery for its plant is not a consumer.</li>
    <li>Limitation ignored — complaints filed beyond two years without a credible application for condonation.</li>
    <li>Jurisdiction computed on the compensation claimed instead of the consideration paid.</li>
    <li>Deficiency pleaded but not proved — no expert report where the defect is technical, no medical opinion in negligence claims.</li>
    <li>Concurrent civil suits — pursuing the same relief in two forums invites dismissal in one.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p>Handled with attention to these points, the consumer jurisdiction remains one of the faster and more economical remedies available to litigants in Delhi &mdash; a forum where a well-documented complaint, filed in time and in the right commission, can produce an enforceable order without the length of an ordinary civil trial.</p>
